 ## Step 2: Install `@angular/localize` (`ng_add_localize`)
 
 ```bash
-npx ng add '@angular/localize@^<angular-major>' --skip-confirmation --use-at-runtime --project <project>
+npx ng add "@angular/localize@$(node -p 'require("@angular/compiler/package.json").version')" --skip-confirmation --use-at-runtime --project <project>
 ```
 
 **Under `globalize-guide` the orchestrator has already run this on the main thread** (`SKILL.md §2.0`), so the install and its lockfile change stay outside the subagent. The subagent does not re-run it: it confirms the edits below and adds whichever are missing. Run the command only when this reference is followed on its own, with no orchestrator.
 
-`<angular-major>` is the major of detection `version` (`22.2` → `^22`); `<project>` is the Step 1 project. With pnpm, Yarn or Bun, run the same command through `pnpm exec`, `yarn` or `bunx`.
+The spec must be the **exact version** of the project's installed `@angular/compiler`; the `node -p` substitution reads it out of `node_modules`, so run the command from the project root. `<project>` is the Step 1 project. With pnpm, Yarn or Bun, run the same command through `pnpm exec`, `yarn` or `bunx` — the substitution is POSIX and needs no quoting change (the single-quote rule in `SKILL.md` §2.0 exists for `^` under zsh, and this spec has no `^`).
 
 - `--use-at-runtime` puts the package in `dependencies` instead of `devDependencies`. Runtime `loadTranslations()` imports it in the browser bundle, so it must be a runtime dependency.
 - `ng add` adds `@angular/localize/init` to the build (and test) target's `polyfills` in `angular.json`, `@angular/localize` to `compilerOptions.types` in `tsconfig.app.json` (and `tsconfig.spec.json`), and a `/// <reference types="@angular/localize" />` line at the top of `src/main.ts`. **Confirm the polyfill and the types entry after it runs and add them if missing** — `$localize` is a global declared by those types; without them every component using it fails to typecheck.
-- **Already applied:** `@angular/localize` is in `dependencies` at the same major as `@angular/core`, and the polyfill is present → skip.
+- **Already applied:** `@angular/localize` is in `dependencies` at the **same version** as `@angular/compiler`, and the polyfill is present → skip. A matching major but a different patch is **not** already-applied — see the pin note below.
 
-**Troubleshooting — the computed pin.** This repo pins every install to a fixed SemVer major. This one is computed from the project instead, because `@angular/localize`'s major must equal `@angular/core`'s: Angular's packages are released in lockstep, and `@angular/localize/tools` (used by the Step 4 converter) is not semver-guaranteed across majors. It is still a caret range. If `@angular/localize` already sits in `devDependencies` at the wrong major, `ng add` at the right major replaces it. Keep it on the same major whenever `ng update` moves `@angular/core`.
+**Troubleshooting — why an exact version, and why neither a caret nor a bare name.** This repo pins every install to a fixed SemVer major. This one is pinned to the project's exact Angular version instead, and the two shorter forms both break.
+
+`@angular/localize` declares its peers as **exact versions, not ranges**: `22.2.1` requires `@angular/compiler@22.2.1` *and* `@angular/compiler-cli@22.2.1`, and every release back to v16 does the same. So:
+
+- **`@angular/localize@^<major>`** resolves to the newest patch in that major (`^22` → `22.2.1`). On a project whose lockfile holds any earlier patch the install fails outright: `npm error ERESOLVE … peer @angular/compiler@"22.2.1" from @angular/localize@22.2.1 … Found: @angular/compiler@22.0.0`. The caret only succeeds when the project happens to sit on the newest patch of its major — which a freshly generated project does and a maintained one usually does not.
+- **a bare `ng add @angular/localize`** is worse, because it fails *silently*. The Angular CLI runs its "Searching for compatible package version" task **only** when the spec is `*`, i.e. only for a bare name. That search tries `latest`, and on a peer conflict sweeps the newest release of each major, newest first. Because every major's newest release carries an exact peer pin, none of them satisfies a project on an older patch — until the sweep reaches `@angular/localize@9.1.13`, the last release that declares **no** `peerDependencies` at all, which it therefore accepts. A 2020 major is installed and its `ng-add` schematic is the one that runs.
+- **`@angular/localize@<installed @angular/compiler version>`** installs cleanly. That is the command above.
+
+`@angular/localize/tools` (the Step 4 converter) is not semver-guaranteed across majors, and the peer pin makes it exact within one, so **re-run this command whenever `ng update` moves Angular**. If `@angular/localize` is already present at a mismatched version — in `dependencies` or `devDependencies` — `ng add` at the exact version replaces it.
 
